NAME
       fdisk - manipulate the GPT partition table of a disk

SYNOPSIS
       **fdisk** __DISK__
       **fdisk** **-l**|**--list** [__DISK__...]

DESCRIPTION
       **fdisk** shows and edits GUID partition tables (GPT). With **-l** it
       prints the table of every disk (or of the named disks) and exits.
       With a __DISK__ name it starts an interactive session. A **/dev/**
       prefix is accepted: **fdisk /dev/sdb** is the same as **fdisk sdb**.

       All changes stay in memory until the **w** command writes them. **q**
       leaves without touching the disk.

       Partitions are named after the disk and the GPT entry number:
       **sdb1** is entry 1 of **sdb**. Use that name with **mkfs**(8).

COMMANDS
       **g**   create a new, empty GPT (a new disk identifier, no partitions)
       **n**   add a partition; asks for the number, the first sector and the
           last sector. The last sector may be given as a size:
           **+500M**, **+2G**, **+1T**, **+4096** (sectors). Defaults take the
           first free, 1 MiB aligned area. New partitions get the type
           **Linux filesystem**.
       **d**   delete a partition
       **t**   change the type of a partition (number or alias, **L** lists)
       **l**   list the known partition types
       **p**   print the partition table
       **w**   write the table to disk and exit
       **q**   quit without saving
       **m**   show the command menu

PARTITION TYPES
         alias  name                     use
         efi    EFI System               UEFI boot partition (FAT32)
         linux  Linux filesystem         ext2 data or root partition
         swap   Linux swap               used as swap at boot
         basic  Microsoft basic data     also mounted as ext2 root
         purec  PureC system (legacy)    older PureC installs

       Unknown types are shown as **unknown** and kept as they are.

WHAT IS WRITTEN
       **w** writes, in this order:

         - the partition entries at sectors 2-33 and the primary GPT header
           at sector 1;
         - the backup entries and the backup header at the end of the disk;
         - a protective MBR entry (type 0xEE) in sector 0.

       The boot code in sector 0 (the first 446 bytes) is kept, so a disk
       that boots through the BIOS stays bootable. Both headers carry
       CRC32 checksums. If the primary GPT is damaged but the backup is
       good, fdisk loads the backup and **w** repairs the primary copy.

       Disks with an old MBR (dos) table are shown read-only; **g** replaces
       the table with GPT. Only 512-byte sectors are supported.

PERMISSIONS
       Reading and writing the disk needs root (uid 0) or the STORAGE_ADMIN
       capability. Without them fdisk reports "permission denied".

NOTES
       The kernel reads partition tables at boot only. After **w**, reboot
       before using the new partitions.

       fdisk changes only the partition table, never the data inside
       partitions. Deleting a partition and creating it again at the same
       sectors keeps its contents.

       Editing the disk that holds the running system is allowed; the new
       layout is used after the next boot. Do not delete or move the root
       partition.

       USB disks cannot be written by fdisk yet; ATA and AHCI (SATA) disks
       can.

EXAMPLES
       Show all disks:

           fdisk -l

       Split a new disk into a 1 GiB swap and an ext2 data partition:

           fdisk sdb
             g
             n   (Enter, Enter, +1G)
             t   (swap)
             n   (Enter, Enter, Enter)
             w
           reboot
           mkfs ext2 sdb2

SEE ALSO
       mkfs(8), df(1)
